---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { labs } from '../data/labs';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const bad = labs.filter((lab) => lab.verdict === 'bad');
const good = labs.filter((lab) => lab.verdict === 'good');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cheat sheet · Booking Experience Lab"
  description="Every pattern from the labs, with the tell and the fix, on one page."
>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
      <h1>The cheat sheet</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every pattern from the labs on one page: what it is, how to spot it from the outside, and
        what to build instead. Spoilers throughout — play the labs first.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="bad-h">
      <h2 id="bad-h" class="group-h">
        <span class="badge badge--bad">Patterns to refuse to ship</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="rows">
        {
          bad.map((lab) => (
            <article class="row card">
              <div class="row__head">
                <h3 class="row__title">{lab.pattern}</h3>
                <a class="row__link" href={`${base}/labs/${lab.slug}`}>
                  Lab: {lab.title} →
                </a>
              </div>
              <div class="row__cols">
                <div>
                  <p class="col-h">The tell</p>
                  <ul>
                    {lab.spot.slice(0, 3).map((item) => (
                      <li>{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <p class="col-h">Build this instead</p>
                  <ul>
                    {lab.fix.slice(0, 3).map((item) => (
                      <li>{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>
              {lab.context && <p class="row__context">{lab.context}</p>}
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="good-h">
      <h2 id="good-h" class="group-h">
        <span class="badge badge--good">Patterns worth copying</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="rows">
        {
          good.map((lab) => (
            <article class="row card">
              <div class="row__head">
                <h3 class="row__title">{lab.pattern}</h3>
                <a class="row__link" href={`${base}/labs/${lab.slug}`}>
                  Lab: {lab.title} →
                </a>
              </div>
              <div class="row__cols">
                <div>
                  <p class="col-h">What makes it work</p>
                  <ul>
                    {lab.spot.slice(0, 3).map((item) => (
                      <li>{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <p class="col-h">Keep doing</p>
                  <ul>
                    {lab.fix.slice(0, 3).map((item) => (
                      <li>{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rule prose">
      <h2>One rule, if you only keep one</h2>
      <p>
        Every pattern in the "refuse to ship" list is the same move: the interface knows something
        the user needs in order to decide, and it withholds, delays, or distorts it until the
        decision is already made. Fake scarcity distorts availability. Drip pricing delays the
        price. Preticked extras distort what you chose. The roach motel withholds the exit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Appointments raise the stakes on all four, because the person booking is often unwell,
        anxious, or working around a job they cannot leave. Friction that merely annoys a shopper
        will make a patient give up, and a patient who gives up on cancelling does not keep the
        slot — they simply do not arrive.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the test is not "does this feel manipulative" — that is unreliable from the inside of a
        team that has stared at the flow for months. The test is: <strong>at the moment the user
        commits, do they know what they are committing to?</strong> If a screen would lose money by
        answering that honestly, that is the finding, and it belongs in front of whoever asked for
        the screen.
      </p>
      <p class="rule__back">
        <a class="btn" href={`${base}/`}>Back to the labs</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .head {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  .group-h {
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .rows {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
  }
  .row {
    padding: 1.15rem 1.25rem;
  }
  .row__head {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 1rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.6rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .row__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }
  .row__link {
    font-size: 0.84rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .row__link:hover {
    color: var(--text);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .row__cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }
  .col-h {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }
  .row__cols ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .row__cols li {
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  .row__context {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    border-top: 1px dashed var(--border);
    font-size: 0.84rem;
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }
  .rule {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .rule__back {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
